  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nThe rendering section uses the word \"expected\" specially, meaning \"must\" for applicable UAs:\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#rendering\n\nIn the following paragraph (and others with \"expected native appearance\" or \"expected primitive appearance\"), it doesn't make sense grammatically to replace expected with must:\n\n> Its expected [native appearance](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-ui/#native-appearance) is to render as an ['inline-block'](https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#value-def-inline-block) box depicting a one-line text control.\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-input-element-as-a-text-entry-widget\n\nMaybe it's OK since the word used is indeed \"expected\", but if one reads \"expected\" as \"must\" then it's a bit jarring or confusing. Maybe reword to \"Its native appearance is expected to be ...\".\n\nRelated: #11644",
